schema: encyclopedia-entry
object:
  title: Metadata That Matters
  type: encyclopedia-entry
  page_type: guide
  summary: >-
    How to design a small, durable set of metadata fields (title, type,
    status, owner, source, updated, tags) so a knowledge commons stays
    findable and trustworthy after the people who built it move on. Draws
    the type-vs-tag distinction (one type per document, many tags, a
    controlled vocabulary to prevent sprawl), recommends borrowing
    established field names (Dublin Core, Murmurations) rather than
    inventing new ones, and shows how to make the schema machine-enforced
    (Astro/Zod content collections, remark-lint-frontmatter-schema) so
    consistency does not depend on memory. Names status, owner, and source
    as the three non-negotiable "trust fields."
  audience: knowledge-commons builders and stewards (any team, not local-node-specific)
  known_tensions:
    - >-
      minimal metadata (fast to fill in, low friction) vs. rich metadata
      (more useful for trust/findability) — resolved as "spend the field
      budget carefully," six or seven fields as a starting default
  related_concepts:
    - Type vs. Tag (metadata discipline)
  related_resources:
    - Dublin Core
    - Murmurations Protocol
  maturity: raw
  public_use: source-linked-unreviewed
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/metadata-that-matters.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted

# assessed-but-excluded:
#   - This article explicitly cites the Regen Web3 Toolkit's own internal
#     architecture ("eleventh cross-cutting principle, type/tag
#     discipline," the v0.1 required-field recommendation) — these are
#     self-references to this same toolkit, not external things to
#     register, so no separate resource/source-system object was created
#     for "ToolkitOntology" / "ToolkitResources."
#   - The concrete YAML frontmatter example and the Zod code snippet in the
#     article were folded into the encyclopedia-entry summary/notes rather
#     than split into an implementation-record — they are illustrative
#     code, not a documented deployment.
